<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-ornithine biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="GLUTORN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; While the amino acid |FRAME: L-ORNITHINE| is not involved directly in protein synthesis, it is an important intermediate in many metabolic pathways, such as the |FRAME:PWY-4984| and the biosynthesis of several compounds, including arginine (see |FRAME:ARGSYN-PWY| and |FRAME:ARGSYNBSUB-PWY|), proline (see |FRAME:PWY-3341| and |FRAME:PWY-4981|), and putrescine (|FRAME:PWY-46|), which leads to the biosynthesis of other polyamines and pyrimidines. It is also involved in |FRAME: PWY-6818|. This pathway documents the synthesis of |FRAME:L-ORNITHINE| from |FRAME:GLT| via &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-acetylated intermediates. The presumed reason for the acetylation is that it prevents the spontaneous cyclization of glutamate derivatives, which leads to proline biosynthesis (see |FRAME:PROSYN-PWY|), thus keeping the pathways leading to arginine and proline separate (|CITS: [12633501]| and references therein). Two alternative pathways have evolved, which differ in the way the key intermediate |FRAME: ACETYL-GLU| (NAG) is formed, and in the way the acetyl group is removed from another intermediate, |FRAME:N-ALPHA-ACETYLORNITHINE|. In this pathway, which is less common, NAG is formed by NAG synthase, and |FRAME:N-ALPHA-ACETYLORNITHINE| is hydrolyzed by the enzyme acetylornithine deacetylase, forming |FRAME: L-ORNITHINE| and |FRAME: ACET|. In the other pathway (see |FRAME:ARGSYNBSUB-PWY|), which is found in most of the prokaryotic and eukaryotic microbes investigated up to now, the two reactions are linked: the acetyl group which is removed from |FRAME: N-ALPHA-ACETYLORNITHINE| is recycled onto |FRAME: GLT|, regenerating |FRAME: ACETYL-GLU| (NAG). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This ornithine biosynthesis pathway has been found in the |FRAME:TAX-543| |FRAME: TAX-83333| and |FRAME: TAX-90371| |CITS: [3534538]|, the |FRAME: TAX-641| |CITS: [10692366]|, the Gram-negative bacterium |FRAME: TAX-34| |CITS: [ 9829957]|, and the archaeon |FRAME: TAX-2287| |CITS: [vandecasteele90]|. In it |FRAME: GLT| is acetylated to NAG by the enzyme |FRAME:N-ACETYLTRANSFER-CPLX|, encoded by the |FRAME:EG10063| gene. The acetyl donor for this reaction is |FRAME: ACETYL-COA|. NAG is then converted in several enzymatic steps to |FRAME: N-ALPHA-ACETYLORNITHINE|. The acetyl group is removed in the form of |FRAME: ACET| by the enzyme |FRAME:ACETYLORNDEACET-CPLX|, encoded by the |FRAME:EG11286| gene, resulting in |FRAME:L-ORNITHINE|.</Description>
